1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,321
There's a real mystery at the
heart of our school system,

2
00:00:03,321 --> 00:00:06,759
and a recent economics study
has tried to make sense of it.

3
00:00:06,759 --> 00:00:11,279
We're talking about thousands of bright students
who, every single year, end up on tracks

4
00:00:11,279 --> 00:00:16,920
that don't make use of their potential at
all. So the central question, the real puzzle,

5
00:00:16,920 --> 00:00:19,331
is this: immigrant kids in vocational school,

6
00:00:19,331 --> 00:00:22,760
is it the teachers' fault? Or is
there something else behind it?

7
00:00:22,760 --> 00:00:26,120
Let's dive straight into the data,
because this is a truly fascinating story.

8
00:00:26,960 --> 00:00:31,280
The paradox jumps out as soon as you look at
the choices made at the end of middle school.

9
00:00:31,280 --> 00:00:33,842
Kids with a migrant background
who have excellent grades,

10
00:00:33,842 --> 00:00:35,460
and I mean grades that are literally

11
00:00:35,460 --> 00:00:38,633
identical to their Italian
classmates', what do they do?

12
00:00:38,633 --> 00:00:41,240
They choose vocational schools far more often.

13
00:00:41,240 --> 00:00:45,640
They basically rule out the liceo or
technical schools from the start. It's crazy.

14
00:00:45,640 --> 00:00:51,121
With the same objective talent, they
almost systematically decide to aim low,

15
00:00:51,121 --> 00:00:54,680
and so they take far less
demanding school tracks.

16
00:00:54,680 --> 00:00:58,560
Among experts, this phenomenon is
called educational segregation.

17
00:00:58,560 --> 00:01:02,548
Put simply, it happens when students
from disadvantaged backgrounds

18
00:01:02,548 --> 00:01:06,000
are channelled en masse into
less demanding school tracks.

19
00:01:06,000 --> 00:01:10,400
So the move to high school turns
into a kind of unintended trap.

20
00:01:10,400 --> 00:01:13,368
Instead of rewarding those who
deserve it, it ends up freezing,

21
00:01:13,368 --> 00:01:15,960
completely locking in, the
initial social inequalities.

22
00:01:15,960 --> 00:01:17,960
A real shame, in short.

23
00:01:17,960 --> 00:01:21,760
The baseline data tell a
truly unambiguous story.

24
00:01:21,760 --> 00:01:26,439
If we look only at boys with
very high ability levels,

25
00:01:26,439 --> 00:01:29,409
those with the top scores
on the standardised tests

26
00:01:29,409 --> 00:01:32,320
at the start of middle
school, we find a huge gap.

27
00:01:32,320 --> 00:01:37,200
Boys with a migrant background, despite
being the stars of the class, are

28
00:01:37,200 --> 00:01:38,560
clearly less likely

29
00:01:38,560 --> 00:01:43,515
than equally bright native classmates to
enrol in a liceo or a technical school.

30
00:01:43,515 --> 00:01:46,200
And comparing the paths of these two groups,

31
00:01:46,200 --> 00:01:49,435
there's one absolutely
crucial point to keep in mind.

32
00:01:49,435 --> 00:01:52,867
The heart of the matter has
nothing, absolutely nothing,

33
00:01:52,867 --> 00:01:56,115
to do with cognitive ability
or level of preparation.

34
00:01:56,115 --> 00:01:59,054
Both groups can solve
exactly the same equations

35
00:01:59,054 --> 00:02:02,115
and understand the same
complex texts, flawlessly.

36
00:02:02,115 --> 00:02:05,280
The real problem is a
kind of invisible barrier,

37
00:02:05,280 --> 00:02:08,314
like driving with the handbrake on, that stops

38
00:02:08,314 --> 00:02:12,314
immigrant kids from matching their
ambitions to their real potential.

39
00:02:12,314 --> 00:02:15,155
But this negative trend can be broken.

40
00:02:15,634 --> 00:02:19,115
To find out, a team of researchers set up
an experiment in dozens of middle schools

41
00:02:19,115 --> 00:02:24,314
in Northern Italy, called the
Equality of Opportunity project.

42
00:02:24,314 --> 00:02:28,914
The goal was crystal clear: to realign these
students' educational horizons with their

43
00:02:28,914 --> 00:02:31,578
true abilities, and to do
it before, long before,

44
00:02:31,578 --> 00:02:34,514
they had to fill in their
high school enrolment forms.

45
00:02:34,514 --> 00:02:37,555
The method used was super rigorous.

46
00:02:37,555 --> 00:02:40,445
In each participating school,
the 10 immigrant students

47
00:02:40,445 --> 00:02:42,914
with the very best test
scores were identified.

48
00:02:42,914 --> 00:02:46,995
They were offered a special programme
lasting a full two years: there was

49
00:02:46,995 --> 00:02:51,834
psychological career counselling and also
tutoring sessions to sharpen their study skills.

50
00:02:51,834 --> 00:02:55,200
They chose to bet everything
on the brightest minds around,

51
00:02:55,200 --> 00:02:59,194
precisely to understand deep down
what was actually holding them back.

52
00:02:59,194 --> 00:03:04,052
The whole project rested on a truly
fascinating psychological theory:

53
00:03:04,052 --> 00:03:06,234
social cognitive career theory.

54
00:03:06,234 --> 00:03:10,474
In short, this approach suggests that
personal goals and, pay attention, above all

55
00:03:10,474 --> 00:03:13,051
self-confidence weigh exactly as much as raw

56
00:03:13,051 --> 00:03:16,155
intelligence when we make
decisions about our future.

57
00:03:16,155 --> 00:03:20,014
In fact, the idea was not at all to push
students into the liceo by force, but

58
00:03:20,014 --> 00:03:23,314
rather to build a deep
awareness of what they could do,

59
00:03:23,314 --> 00:03:26,314
helping them truly believe
in their own resources.

60
00:03:26,314 --> 00:03:28,414
And here comes an incredible twist:

61
00:03:28,414 --> 00:03:32,194
digging through the results of
the programme's different parts,

62
00:03:32,194 --> 00:03:34,954
a completely unexpected detail emerged.

63
00:03:34,954 --> 00:03:38,719
Even though the kids got solid
practical help with study skills,

64
00:03:38,719 --> 00:03:41,955
the real driver of change
wasn't the academic tutoring.

65
00:03:41,955 --> 00:03:44,730
Not at all: the researchers
found that the real spark

66
00:03:44,730 --> 00:03:47,715
came from the psychological
and motivational counselling.

67
00:03:47,715 --> 00:03:51,072
This clearly shows that
the hurdle wasn't technical

68
00:03:51,072 --> 00:03:54,034
but entirely mental: a matter of aspirations.

69
00:03:54,034 --> 00:03:56,393
At this point the question naturally arises:

70
00:03:56,393 --> 00:03:59,235
can such a targeted
intervention, we're talking about

71
00:03:59,235 --> 00:04:02,689
a handful of hours of psychological
counselling in the end,

72
00:04:02,689 --> 00:04:05,675
really close such a deep
and entrenched social gap?

73
00:04:06,075 --> 00:04:09,835
Can a simple space to reflect on one's
ambitions change the school destiny

74
00:04:09,835 --> 00:04:15,035
of kids whom the system had already, in
a way, benched onto secondary tracks?

75
00:04:15,035 --> 00:04:19,355
The results for the boys involved
were nothing short of extraordinary.

76
00:04:19,355 --> 00:04:25,754
The probability that they chose a much more
demanding high school shot up, by about a tenth.

77
00:04:25,754 --> 00:04:29,074
This leap completely, and I stress completely,

78
00:04:29,074 --> 00:04:32,754
wiped out the initial gap
with their Italian peers.

79
00:04:33,314 --> 00:04:37,314
In practice, in one fell swoop, the
programme put these talented students back

80
00:04:37,314 --> 00:04:40,795
on exactly the same school path
as their Italian-born classmates,

81
00:04:40,795 --> 00:04:43,954
equalising opportunities for
those with the same potential.

82
00:04:43,954 --> 00:04:44,954
Crazy, right?

83
00:04:44,954 --> 00:04:49,514
And mind you, the benefits
didn't stop there, at enrolment.

84
00:04:49,514 --> 00:04:55,034
In the following years, grade retention
rates for these same boys practically halved.

85
00:04:55,034 --> 00:04:57,034
They plunged by almost half.

86
00:04:57,034 --> 00:05:00,208
This is a remarkable finding, because
it proves once and for all that

87
00:05:00,208 --> 00:05:03,475
they were perfectly capable of handling
a really tough course of study.

88
00:05:03,475 --> 00:05:07,635
They were by no means biting off more
than they could chew after the programme.

89
00:05:07,635 --> 00:05:13,155
Quite simply, before the intervention,
they were aiming far too low.

90
00:05:13,155 --> 00:05:18,395
But hold on tight, because the data
delivered a serious plot twist.

91
00:05:18,395 --> 00:05:21,520
When they measured the
programme's impact on the girls,

92
00:05:21,520 --> 00:05:24,475
well, it turned out the
effect was practically zero.

93
00:05:24,475 --> 00:05:26,475
That's right, zero.

94
00:05:26,475 --> 00:05:31,235
For girls, the intervention didn't shift
school choices or later performance one bit.

95
00:05:31,235 --> 00:05:33,681
A gender difference that leaves you stunned,

96
00:05:33,681 --> 00:05:36,795
but that actually hides a
perfectly logical explanation.

97
00:05:36,795 --> 00:05:40,471
The reason the girls didn't change course is,

98
00:05:40,471 --> 00:05:44,555
deep down, great news:
they didn't need to at all.

99
00:05:44,555 --> 00:05:48,107
Their school choices were
already precisely aligned with

100
00:05:48,107 --> 00:05:51,595
their high abilities, well
before the experiment began.

101
00:05:51,595 --> 00:05:56,395
Unlike the boys, who massively underestimated
themselves, girls with a migrant

102
00:05:56,395 --> 00:06:00,123
background and top grades were
already choosing leading licei and

103
00:06:00,123 --> 00:06:03,794
technical schools, exactly as
often as their Italian classmates.

104
00:06:03,794 --> 00:06:08,154
So what triggered such a
powerful reaction in the boys?

105
00:06:08,154 --> 00:06:11,674
Basically, the intervention
flipped four key switches.

106
00:06:11,674 --> 00:06:14,634
First, it pumped up their general aspirations.

107
00:06:14,634 --> 00:06:17,674
Second, it supercharged their
confidence in their own abilities.

108
00:06:17,674 --> 00:06:21,356
Third, it crushed the fear of
the usual external obstacles,

109
00:06:21,356 --> 00:06:23,914
like money problems or fear of prejudice.

110
00:06:24,155 --> 00:06:29,275
And finally, amazingly, it even
improved their scores on later tests.

111
00:06:29,275 --> 00:06:32,555
In short, their horizons
literally opened wide.

112
00:06:32,555 --> 00:06:34,980
But there's another vital
piece of this puzzle,

113
00:06:34,980 --> 00:06:38,075
and it concerns the adults who
live in the school ecosystem:

114
00:06:38,075 --> 00:06:39,075
the teachers.

115
00:06:39,075 --> 00:06:44,075
They hold the keys to the system, and
it's fascinating to see how the perception

116
00:06:44,075 --> 00:06:48,754
teachers had of these students changed,
for the better, during the experiment.

117
00:06:48,754 --> 00:06:52,212
This means the inner change
the boys were going through

118
00:06:52,212 --> 00:06:55,355
was picked up loud and
clear from the outside too.

119
00:06:55,355 --> 00:06:58,458
As we know, in Italy middle
school teachers issue

120
00:06:58,458 --> 00:07:01,435
a formal track recommendation
before enrolment.

121
00:07:01,435 --> 00:07:05,683
Well, it turned out teachers became
far more likely to recommend a liceo or

122
00:07:05,683 --> 00:07:09,875
a technical school precisely to the
boys who had attended the counselling.

123
00:07:09,875 --> 00:07:13,507
We're talking about a jump in recommendations
for ambitious tracks:

124
00:07:13,507 --> 00:07:15,787
a huge leap in teachers' expectations.

125
00:07:16,426 --> 00:07:19,474
And the smartest part of this whole dynamic?

126
00:07:19,474 --> 00:07:23,907
Teachers weren't simply rubber-stamping
everyone in the project.

127
00:07:23,907 --> 00:07:26,307
If that were the case,
they'd have inflated the

128
00:07:26,307 --> 00:07:28,707
recommendations for girls
too. But they didn't.

129
00:07:28,707 --> 00:07:33,027
Instead, they updated their assessments
in a very precise and selective way,

130
00:07:33,027 --> 00:07:36,349
noticing the new motivation
and the real progress

131
00:07:36,349 --> 00:07:39,467
that only the boys were showing at that point.

132
00:07:39,467 --> 00:07:44,546
In short, they recognised and encouraged
a potential that was finally surfacing.

133
00:07:44,626 --> 00:07:49,467
And to top it all off, there's also a fantastic
bonus effect that emerged from the data.

134
00:07:49,467 --> 00:07:52,760
Immigrant classmates who
weren't directly part of

135
00:07:52,760 --> 00:07:55,986
the programme started
getting better grades too.

136
00:07:55,986 --> 00:07:57,818
A genuine positive contagion,

137
00:07:57,818 --> 00:08:01,986
born quite simply from sharing the
same classroom every single day

138
00:08:01,986 --> 00:08:06,546
with classmates who were pulling a brand-new
motivation and self-awareness out of the hat.

139
00:08:06,546 --> 00:08:09,347
Enthusiasm, it seems, is contagious.

140
00:08:09,347 --> 00:08:13,347
To sum up and get to the heart of
this study, we could put it like this.

141
00:08:13,387 --> 00:08:16,461
People with all the right
credentials but shaky self-esteem

142
00:08:16,461 --> 00:08:19,587
end up ruling out the most
ambitious options from the start.

143
00:08:19,587 --> 00:08:21,106
It's sad, but that's how it is.

144
00:08:21,106 --> 00:08:22,973
Wasted talent, in so many cases,

145
00:08:22,973 --> 00:08:26,707
is nothing but the fever of an
immune system made of insecurity.

146
00:08:26,707 --> 00:08:30,122
Perhaps combined with a fixed
idea, internalised over time,

147
00:08:30,122 --> 00:08:34,347
that certain prestigious goals are an
exclusive club reserved for others.

148
00:08:34,347 --> 00:08:36,997
And this leads to a pretty
tough reflection, I think,

149
00:08:36,997 --> 00:08:40,347
for anyone who cares even a little
about the future of our society.

150
00:08:40,347 --> 00:08:44,947
How do we unlock the true potential of every
single mind that walks into those classrooms?

151
00:08:44,947 --> 00:08:48,488
Think about it: if a handful
of hours of good guidance can

152
00:08:48,488 --> 00:08:51,907
literally rewrite the school
destiny of bright students,

153
00:08:51,907 --> 00:08:55,368
wiping out decades-old
structural gaps in one go,

154
00:08:55,368 --> 00:08:59,747
how much human capital are we
throwing away every single year?

155
00:08:59,747 --> 00:09:05,787
Aligning the fire inside us with the talent we
have isn't just a win for the individual kid.

156
00:09:05,787 --> 00:09:09,198
It's the very first, fundamental
step towards building

157
00:09:09,198 --> 00:09:12,987
opportunities that are genuinely,
deeply equal for everyone.
